Brute-forcing in the context of computer security refers to a trial-and-error method used to decode encrypted data such as passwords or Data Encryption Standard (DES) keys. The term "brute force" comes from the idea of trying every possible combination until the correct one is found.

In JavaScript, you can implement a simple brute-force algorithm to guess a password by trying every possible combination of characters until the correct one is found. However, it's important to note that brute-forcing passwords is generally considered unethical and illegal without proper authorization. This example is for educational purposes only.

Here's a basic example of a brute-force password cracker in JavaScript:

```javascript
const targetPassword = 'password123'; // The password you want to crack
const charset = 'abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyzABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZ0123456789'; // The set of characters to try
const charsetLength = charset.length;

function bruteForcePassword(target, charset, charsetLength) {
  for (let i = 0; i < Math.pow(charsetLength, 4); i++) { // Assuming a 4-character password
    let password = '';
    for (let j = 0; j < 4; j++) {
      password += charset.charAt(i % charsetLength);
      i = Math.floor(i / charsetLength);
    }
    if (password === target) {
      console.log(`Password found: ${password}`);
      return password;
    }
  }
  console.log('Password not found.');
}

bruteForcePassword(targetPassword, charset, charsetLength);
```

In this example, the `bruteForcePassword` function tries every possible combination of characters from the `charset` for a 4-character password. It uses a nested loop to generate all possible combinations and checks if any of them match the `targetPassword`. If a match is found, it prints the found password; otherwise, it prints "Password not found."

Keep in mind that this is a very basic example and doesn't include optimizations or more complex scenarios. In real-world applications, brute-forcing passwords is not only slow and inefficient but also highly discouraged and potentially illegal. Always use secure and ethical practices when dealing with passwords and encryption.

