In C, the best way to return an error is by using a combination of return codes and possibly a global error variable or a function that returns a pointer to an error message. Here's a simple example using return codes:

```c
#include <stdio.h>

#define SUCCESS 0
#define ERROR -1

int myFunction() {
    // Your code here
    // If something goes wrong, return ERROR
    return ERROR;
}

int main() {
    int result = myFunction();
    if (result == ERROR) {
        printf("An error occurred.\n");
    } else {
        printf("Function executed successfully.\n");
    }
    return 0;
}
```

In this example, `myFunction` returns `0` on success and `-1` on error. The caller checks the return value and handles the error accordingly.

For more complex error handling, you might want to use a struct to return both the error code and a message:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>

typedef struct {
    int code;
    char message[256];
} Error;

Error myFunction() {
    Error error;
    // Your code here
    // If something goes wrong, set the error code and message
    error.code = ERROR;
    strcpy(error.message, "An error occurred.");
    return error;
}

int main() {
    Error result = myFunction();
    if (result.code == ERROR) {
        printf("Error: %s\n", result.message);
    } else {
        printf("Function executed successfully.\n");
    }
    return 0;
}
```

In this second example, `myFunction` returns an `Error` struct containing both the error code and a message. The caller can then check the code and print the message if needed.

